 \begin{abstract}
-Manipulation requires selective contact: a robot must touch the intended object while avoiding the surfaces around it.
-Localizing that object is insufficient. A correct target identity can still yield an unsafe contact exception if its
-geometric extent absorbs a nearby obstacle, especially under occlusion. We present \sys, a method that converts the
-internal signals of a frozen vision-language-action (VLA) policy and calibrated depth into task-conditioned 3D contact
-geometry. Its central principle is to separate \emph{which object is intended}, \emph{which observed surfaces belong to
-it}, and \emph{which links may touch those surfaces}. Attention selects the target; visibility-aware geometric and
- temporal evidence delimit its contact region without removing uncertain neighboring surfaces. The recovered geometry
-configures link-specific collision queries and changes from a scene object to attached geometry after a confirmed
-grasp. A constrained refinement layer checks the action prefix actually sent to the robot. The policy remains frozen,
-and target geometry requires no external segmentation network. Our planned evaluation will isolate identification,
-extent, and contact handling, and assess their effects on task completion, forbidden contact, and execution cost.
+A manipulation policy must touch its target while preserving nearby obstacles as collision geometry. This requires more than identifying the right object: a coarse target region can also exempt the wrong surface. We introduce \sys, a framework for grounding observed target surfaces from a frozen vision-language-action policy and calibrated depth, without an external segmentation model. Its central design separates semantic selection, surface membership, and contact permission. Noun attention provides sparse seeds; a multi-view depth graph recovers spatial extent through observed surface continuity; internal visual features are evaluated separately as temporal identity checks. A surface-matched robot filter preserves objects between the fingers, and a contact interface restricts target exceptions to designated links and task states. On 180 selected frames from 32 development episodes, the surface method improves episode-mean 3D IoU from 0.837 to 0.893 under the same robot mask. Controlled feature ablations show that direct feature fusion provides no consistent boundary improvement, motivating a distinct role for identity consistency. These development results motivate independent evaluation of grounding generalization and closed-loop contact benefits.
 \end{abstract}
