 \section{Introduction}
-\label{sec:introduction}
+\label{sec:intro}
+To pick up an apple beside a post, a robot must allow its fingers to touch the apple while keeping the post, wrist, and arm subject to collision constraints. After grasping, the apple itself becomes geometry that must avoid the environment. The object identity is unchanged, but the permitted interaction changes. This creates a visual grounding problem with a physical consequence: \emph{which observed surfaces belong to the object that may be contacted, and which must remain protected?}
 
-A robot picking an apple must make contact with the apple and avoid the post beside it. Its fingers may touch the
-fruit; its wrist must not strike either object. Once grasped, the apple becomes part of the moving geometry, and after
-release it returns to the scene. The physical surface is the same, but its role in collision checking changes with
-the task, the robot link, and the state of interaction. \emph{Where a robot should look is therefore different from
-where it may touch.}
+Vision-language-action (VLA) policies provide task-conditioned action proposals~\cite{black2024pi0,intelligence2025pi05}. Geometric wrappers can refine those proposals, but their behavior depends on the geometry supplied to them. Selecting the correct object is insufficient if its bounding volume includes a neighboring obstacle. Likewise, removing a conservative robot volume can erase a held object between the fingers. The relevant error is therefore not only a wrong target identity, but also a wrong assignment of scene surfaces to a contact exception.
 
-Vision-language-action (VLA) policies generate manipulation actions from images and instructions
-\cite{black2024pi0,intelligence2025pi05,nvidia2025gr00t}. Geometric refinement can expose surrounding surfaces as explicit
-constraints on those actions~\cite{wu2025momanipvla,sundaralingam2026curobov2}. Connecting these representations introduces
-a difficult ambiguity. Treating every surface as forbidden blocks intentional grasp contact. Removing the target from
-a shared collision map permits contact by links that should avoid it. Worse, identifying the correct apple does not
-guarantee that the region removed from the map contains only the apple. A coarse bounding volume, a merged point-cloud
-component, or a stale estimate under occlusion can silently erase the nearby post.
+Recent work makes this distinction increasingly pertinent. KNOWS already extracts target information from internal VLA attention and combines it with externally segmented geometry~\cite{park2026knows}. RoboGround and OBEYED-VLA show the value of object-focused intermediate representations for robot policies~\cite{huang2025roboground,vo2026obeyed}. AFI uses spatial affordance fields to intervene on VLA execution~\cite{xu2026afi}. These methods motivate grounding for action, but do not make generic visual segmentation equivalent to contact authorization. Conversely, a segmentation error is not evidence that an external segmenter is intrinsically unsuitable: perception and contact handling must be compared under matched downstream conditions.
 
-This is a problem of \emph{contact-target extent}, beyond target localization. Attention-guided target selection already
-appears in KNOWS, which ranks objects supplied by an external segmenter~\cite{park2026knows}; external grounding and
-target-mask exclusion also support collision-aware execution~\cite{hu2026vlsa,wu2025momanipvla}. These ideas establish
-useful interfaces between semantics and geometry. Our focus is the next step: recovering observed target extent from
-the policy's own signals and depth, maintaining its boundary during partial visibility, and translating that extent
-into selective contact constraints.
+We introduce \sys\ to study the interface between policy-internal visual evidence and observed 3D contact geometry. Our design assigns different responsibilities to signals with different spatial resolution. Noun attention identifies promising target patches. Depth supplies the observed surface topology needed to distinguish neighboring objects. A graph energy propagates seed evidence across those surfaces, while explicit support negatives and region selection limit target extent. Robot pixels are removed only when rendered robot depth agrees with the observation. The output is an observed surface region, rather than a ball interpreted as object membership.
 
-We present \sys\ (Fig.~\ref{fig:teaser}), built around three distinct decisions: \emph{identity}, \emph{extent}, and
-\emph{permission}. VLA attention provides evidence for identity. Multi-view geometry determines which surfaces support
-that identity, while temporal memory preserves the object through occlusion without freezing an incorrect boundary.
-Only supported target geometry receives a contact exception; neighboring and ambiguous surfaces remain obstacles.
-Finally, configured contact rules determine which links can use that exception. An attention score never directly
-deletes an obstacle or authorizes a robot link.
+An initially appealing alternative is to strengthen this graph with internal visual-feature similarity. Our development experiments do not support that assumption. With the improved geometric pipeline held fixed, feature edges have little effect for four of five tested representations, while prototype unaries often remove valid target surfaces or favor the wrong component. The diagnostics point to a mismatch between patch-level descriptors and fine surface boundaries. This motivates using features at a different level: a region descriptor can check temporal identity consistency without dictating each voxel's membership. The latter use remains exploratory, particularly because the present confidence analysis contains only six wrong-choice events.
 
-This separation also resolves a temporal tension. Stable manipulation requires remembering the target when the hand
-occludes it, yet persistent memory must not preserve an obstacle accidentally merged into the target. \sys\ therefore
-maintains identity and surface membership separately: identity can persist while newly visible geometry corrects its
-extent. A lightweight sub-task probe on frozen VLA features guides identity updates, while physical grasp feedback
-controls attachment and release. The same target geometry then supports approach, carrying, and placement without
-becoming a permanent hole in the collision map.
-
-Our contributions are organized around this representation and its consequences:
+Our contributions are threefold:
 \begin{itemize}
-\item \textbf{Boundary-preserving 3D target grounding.} We combine VLA attention with multi-view geometry and temporal
-membership updates to construct an observed contact region without an external segmentation network. The formulation
-separates identity persistence from extent correction and retains ambiguous neighboring surfaces.
-\item \textbf{Grounding-to-contact constraints.} We convert this region into link- and phase-specific collision queries,
-including a grasp-confirmed transfer to attached geometry, and use them to refine and check the executed action prefix.
-The connection makes grounding errors explicit at the point where they alter permissible motion.
-\item \textbf{A mechanism-focused evaluation design.} We specify paired experiments that isolate target identification,
-extent estimation, and contact handling under a common controller. These experiments will establish which changes
-improve safe task completion and which merely increase stopping; the empirical contribution awaits their results.
+\item An attention-seeded observed-surface construction that combines depth-continuous connectivity, patch-mass-preserving seeds, support negatives, and surface-matched robot removal without fitting a segmentation model or updating the VLA.
+\item A controlled analysis of where internal features help or fail: matched-mask extent comparisons, feature-edge and feature-unary ablations, boundary diagnostics, and exploratory temporal identity checks.
+\item An explicit interface from supported target surfaces to link-specific contact exceptions, together with an evaluation design that separates grounding quality from downstream contact outcomes.
 \end{itemize}
-
-The underlying VLA is frozen after embodiment adaptation. A small phase probe is trained separately, and internal
-signals are read through an additional extraction pass. Thus, our claim concerns constructing contact geometry from
-a frozen policy, rather than training a new collision-avoidance policy or eliminating all auxiliary learning.
+The current empirical scope is offline development evaluation. Under the same rendered robot mask, the proposed extent pipeline improves 3D IoU by 5.7 percentage points over radius growth and clustering. The contact interface defines the intended downstream use; this result does not establish collision reduction or safe task completion.
