 \section{Conclusion}
-\sys\ connects frozen VLA representations to selective physical interaction through task-conditioned 3D contact
-geometry. Its organizing principle separates intended identity, supported extent, and permitted contact. Multi-view
-and temporal evidence maintain a correctable target boundary; link-specific queries and physical grasp feedback
-carry that geometry through approach, transport, and release. The resulting method makes the spatial consequences
-of grounding explicit in the constraints applied to executed actions. The planned evaluation will determine when
-this representation improves safe task completion and quantify the limits imposed by visibility, geometry coverage,
-and computation. Comparative findings remain to be established.
+\sys\ grounds observed target surfaces by assigning semantic selection to internal attention and spatial membership to depth continuity. Matched-mask development comparisons support this construction, while feature ablations show that more semantic similarity does not automatically yield a better boundary. Temporal feature agreement and target--hand geometry offer distinct, auxiliary uses of the extracted representations. The resulting surface representation provides a concrete interface for selective contact; independent evaluation must establish whether its offline gains translate into safer execution.
