 \section{Limitations}
-The method depends on calibrated, synchronized depth and a collision model that covers the evaluated robot surfaces.
-Unseen obstacles, calibration error, and geometry outside that coverage remain failure modes. Surface membership
-estimates observed extent; they do not reconstruct hidden object shape. A fully occluded or inseparable target can
-therefore cause conservative stopping, and the current formulation does not actively move cameras to resolve it.
-Contact-link and destination rules are configured rather than learned, limiting transfer to interactions requiring
-new contact semantics. Attention selection and probe calibration may also need adaptation across policies and
-embodiments. Finally, sampled kinematic clearance is not a force constraint or a continuous-time safety certificate;
-latency, compliant contact, and dynamic obstacles require additional treatment. The prospective improvements in this
-draft require implementation-parity checks and the planned experiments before performance conclusions can be drawn.
+The current evidence uses simulated replay depth and selected moments from 32 development episodes. Adaptive method selection, sparse temporal sampling, and six wrong-choice events limit generalization and confidence conclusions. We reconstruct observed surfaces only: occlusion, missing depth, calibration error, and sub-voxel contacts remain unresolved. The graph does not complete hidden geometry or guarantee a correct boundary. Robot-depth matching relies on accurate meshes and kinematics. Auxiliary phase models require supervision, and their labels describe a restricted pick--place sequence. The proposed contact interface has not yet been evaluated with the new surface output in closed loop; neither physical safety, runtime suitability, nor superiority to external segmentation has been established.
